    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d857">

                <pb facs="0806" n="794" sameAs="frus:frus1939v01/pg794"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">300.115(39) Benecke, Alexander/6: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Kennedy</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-09-28T19:00:00+01:00">September 28,
                            1939—7 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received September 28—2:16 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1858. Your 1083, September 27, 11 a.m. Ministry of Economic Warfare stated that
                    the British Government is taking no action whatever <pb facs="0807" n="795"
                        xml:id="pg795"/> at present against exports direct or indirect from Germany
                    to neutral countries carried on neutral ships, the only exception being arms and
                    munitions which there might be reason to believe it was intended to transfer to
                    a German raider.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kennedy</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
